      "text": "**Offline File‑Standardization Pipeline – Overview**\n\nThe pipeline is implemented in a single Python script, `process_pipeline.py`, located in the workspace root.  \nIt scans the directory tree under `/workspace/in/raw/` recursively, processes only files with the extensions `.txt`, `.csv`, or `.json` (case‑insensitive), and writes all transformed artefacts to `/workspace/out/normalized/`. Two additional CSV logs are produced in `/workspace/out/`:\n\n| Log | Purpose | Header |\n|-----|---------|--------|\n| `rename_log.csv` | Records every successful transformation | `source,target,action` |\n| `error_report.csv` | Records malformed records and unsupported files | `source,row_or_record,error_type,details` |\n\nThe script follows the exact rules you specified:\n\n1. **Revenue TXT files**  \n   * Expected format: `Key: Value` lines (e.g., `region: US`, `amount: 1234.56`, `currency: USD`).  \n   * Extracts `region`, `amount`, and `currency`.  \n   * Converts `amount` to a numeric value.  \n   * Emits a JSON array of objects.  \n   * Output filename: `revenue_<yyyy>_<mm>.json` (year and month are parsed from the source filename).  \n\n2. **Customer CSV exports**  \n   * Reads any CSV with a header row.  \n   * Normalises keys to `name`, `email`, `spend`.  \n   * Converts `spend` to a float.  \n   * Emits a JSON array of objects.  \n   * Skips rows that cannot be parsed and records them in `error_report.csv`.  \n\n3. **Inventory JSON files**  \n   * Expects a top‑level key `items` containing an array of objects with `sku` and `qty`.  \n   * Emits a CSV with columns `sku,qty`, sorted by `sku`.  \n   * Output filename: `inventory_<warehouse>.csv` where `<warehouse>` is derived from the source filename.  \n\n**Filename Normalisation & Conflict Resolution**\n\n* All output filenames are converted to lowercase snake_case.  \n* If two source files would produce the same target name, the first (alphabetically by source path) keeps the base name; subsequent ones receive suffixes `__2`, `__3`, … appended before the extension.  \n\n**Logging**\n\n* `rename_log.csv` contains one row per successful transformation, sorted by the source path.  \n* `error_report.csv` contains one row per malformed record or unsupported file, with a descriptive error type and details.  \n\n**Execution**\n\n```bash\npython3 /workspace/process_pipeline.py\n```\n\nThe script creates the output directory and the two log files automatically.  \nIf the input tree is empty or contains no supported files, the logs will be created but contain only the headers.\n\n**Sample Log Entries**\n\n*`rename_log.csv`* (after processing a revenue file and a customer CSV)\n\n```\nsource,target,action\nin/raw/revenue_jan.txt,out/normalized/revenue_2026_09.json,transformed\nin/raw/customers.csv,out/normalized/customers.json,transformed\n```\n\n*`error_report.csv`* (after encountering a malformed CSV row)\n\n```\nsource,row_or_record,error_type,details\nin/raw/customers.csv,row 5,InvalidRow,Missing required fields\n```\n\n**What to Do Next**\n\n1. Place your raw files under `/workspace/in/raw/`.  \n2. Run the script as shown above.  \n3. Inspect `/workspace/out/normalized/` for the transformed artefacts.  \n4. Review the two log files for any issues.\n\nFeel free to adjust the script (e.g., add more validation rules) or integrate it into a larger build pipeline.",
